===Etymology 2===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|hi|चेला}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|/ˈt͡ʃei.lə/|lang=en}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|s|chele}}

# A [[pupil]] or [[disciple]], especially in [[Hinduism]].
#* '''1987''', Phirozshah Dorabji Mehta, ''The Heart of Religion'', [http://books.google.com/books?id=gor7mhCDzAkC&pg=PA36&dq=%22chela%22&hl=en&ei=PNgNTt_DHq7LmAW91-HKDg&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=9&ved=0CFAQ6AEwCDgK#v=onepage&q=%22chela%22&f=false page 36],
#*: The guru had insight. He understood his '''chela'''. The relationship between a guru and his '''chela''' was as a father to a son, as a whole person to a whole person, individual and unique.
#* '''2002''', ''Warrior of Light: The Life of Nicholas Roerich: Artist, Himalayan Explorer, and Visionary'', [http://books.google.com/books?id=4aB_qCOhJroC&pg=PA52&dq=%22chela%22+guru&hl=en&ei=quANTtn2OKzomAWDqOWrDg&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=5&ved=0CD0Q6AEwBDgU#v=onepage&q=%22chela%22%20guru&f=false page 52],
#*: The guru-'''chela''' relationship was a theme in many of Nicholas's paintings. In ''Pearl of Searching'', the guru is looking thoughtfully at a pearl necklace with his '''chela'''. This symbolizes the "pearl of great price" that gives purpose to life.
#* '''2007''', Michael Bertiaux, ''The Voudon Gnostic Workbook: Expanded Edition'', [http://books.google.com/books?id=PHDtPvR3ABgC&pg=PA455&dq=%22chela%22+guru&hl=en&ei=S90NTr3BDKHomAXGq-zJDg&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=9&ved=0CFcQ6AEwCA#v=onepage&q=%22chela%22%20guru&f=false page 455],
#*: This, of course, is done by the guru as the result of a long process in which his consciousness must merge with that of the '''chela''' and wherein the '''chela''' is absorbed essentially into the guru.

